{{also|equivócate}}
==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|æquivocate}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{m|la|aequivocātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|aequivocō||I am called by the same name}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|aequivocus||ambiguous, equivocal}}: compare {{cog|fr|équivoquer}}. See {{m|en|equivocal}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈkwɪvəˌkeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-equivocate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|equivocat}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To use words of equivocal or doubtful signification; to express one's opinions in terms which admit of different senses, with intent to deceive; to use ambiguous expressions with a view to mislead; as, to equivocate is the work of duplicity.
#* '''1687''', {{w|Edward Stillingfleet}}, ''The Unreasonableness of Separation: Or, An Impartial Account of the History, Nature and Pleas of the Present Separation from the Communion of the Church of England''
#*: ''All that Garnet had to say for him was that he supposed he meant to '''equivocate'''.''
# To render equivocal or ambiguous.
#*{{quote-book|en|author=George Buck|title=Reign of Richard the Third|year=1647|passage=He '''equivocated''' his vow by a mental reservation}}

